# Checked-in, deterministic fleet-simulator scenario for deploy/demo.sh
# (talks/talk1-edge-intelligence/TODO-DEMO-RECORDING.md's "Script to write"
# section). Sourced by demo.sh as shell variables, translated into fleet-simulate
# CLI flags -- no fleet-simulator code changes needed for this determinism, just
# these existing flags plus a fixed --seed.
#
# Single truck (truck-47, --fleet-size 1's default id), forced onto I-95N with
# an incident starting in the first few ticks -- see shared/fleet-simulator/src/
# fleet_simulator/fleet.py's forced_ids / force_incident_start_tick logic, which
# only fires when --incident-corridor is paired with --incident-trucks >= 1.
#
# Why this one scenario is expected to produce BOTH outcomes the recording
# needs (a card raised to "high" and uplinked, and at least one card that stays
# local) -- a documented hypothesis about this scenario's shape, not a
# scripted/forced result (see below):
# - severity_classifier.classify_severity only returns "high" directly from
#   abs_engaged or peak_deceleration_g > 0.45, neither of which
#   fleet-simulator's TruckState.step() ever sets on a TelemetryEvent (both
#   stay at their schema defaults, 0.0 / False). So baseline_severity out of
#   this simulator is always "medium" (once stop_go_index climbs past 0.8,
#   which the incident's oscillating speed plus brake/downshift bursts are
#   expected to reach) or "low" -- never "high" on its own.
# - truck-47 has pre-seeded, escalate-worthy trip context (heavy rain, wet
#   asphalt; Liquids / Chemical Tanker cargo; running behind schedule -- see
#   trip_context.py), the same fixture tests/model/eval_lib.py's
#   ESCALATION_SCENARIOS["escalate-worthy"] uses to expect a "raise" decision.
# - So the expectation is: early incident ticks (stop_go_index still low,
#   baseline "low") mostly stay local; ticks once stop_go_index crosses 0.8
#   (baseline "medium") are the ones with a real chance of the LLM raising to
#   "high" and the uplink gate letting the card through.
# - This is a real model decision each time, not a forced one -- escalation
#   isn't scripted for the demo. If a specific take doesn't produce a "high"
#   card, re-run (same seed, same scenario file) rather than fabricating one;
#   per docs/CANON.md, cheap math decides what it decides and the LLM decides
#   what it decides, nothing is faked for the recording.

FLEET_SIZE=1
INCIDENT_CORRIDOR=I-95N
INCIDENT_TRUCKS=1
SEED=4747
RATE=4
DURATION=30
